### Using Snort on Ubuntu to Test Firewall Bypass

You can use Snort 3 to test whether a firewall is properly enforcing its security rules, detect unexpected traffic and identify possible firewall bypass attempts.

The important distinction is that Snort is an IDS/IPS, not a firewall-testing tool by itself. You use Snort alongside firewall rules, packet captures and connection tests to establish whether traffic is being blocked or allowed unexpectedly.

Only perform these tests on your own computer, network or an environment where you have permission.

### 1\. Different ways to test firewall enforcement

Here are the main testing categories.

| Test                         | What you investigate                                  |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Port testing                 | Whether prohibited ports are accessible               |
| TCP testing                  | Whether unauthorised TCP connections pass             |
| UDP testing                  | Whether restricted UDP traffic passes                 |
| ICMP testing                 | Whether ping traffic is allowed unexpectedly          |
| DNS testing                  | Whether DNS restrictions are enforced                 |
| HTTP testing                 | Whether web traffic follows firewall policy           |
| HTTPS testing                | Whether encrypted web connections follow policy       |
| SSH testing                  | Whether remote administration is restricted           |
| IPv6 testing                 | Whether IPv6 bypasses IPv4 firewall rules             |
| Fragmentation testing        | Whether fragmented packets are handled correctly      |
| VPN testing                  | Whether unauthorised VPN connections are permitted    |
| Network segmentation testing | Whether one network segment can access another        |
| Egress testing               | Whether restricted outbound connections are possible  |
| Ingress testing              | Whether unauthorised inbound connections are possible |

### 2\. Prepare Ubuntu

Check your Snort version:

```bash
snort -V
```

Identify your network interfaces:

```bash
ip addr
```

Check your default gateway:

```bash
ip route
```

Check the firewall:

```bash
sudo ufw status verbose
```

For systems using nftables:

```bash
sudo nft list ruleset
```

For systems using iptables:

```bash
sudo iptables -L -n -v
```

These commands help you understand which firewall policies are active.

### 3\. Capture traffic with Snort

Start Snort on your active interface:

```bash
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.lua -i eth0 -A alert_fast
```

Replace `eth0` with your actual interface.

To monitor a saved packet capture:

```bash
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.lua -r firewall-test.pcap
```

To test your configuration:

```bash
sudo snort -T -c /etc/snort/snort.lua
```

### 4\. Create Snort rules for firewall monitoring

Create a custom rules file:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/snort/firewall.rules
```

Add these defensive monitoring rules.

```snort
# 1. Detect ICMP traffic
alert icmp any any -> any any (msg:"ICMP traffic observed"; sid:1000201; rev:1;)

# 2. Detect DNS traffic
alert udp any any -> any 53 (msg:"DNS traffic observed"; sid:1000202; rev:1;)

# 3. Detect SSH traffic
alert tcp any any -> any 22 (msg:"SSH traffic observed"; sid:1000203; rev:1;)

# 4. Detect HTTP traffic
alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg:"HTTP traffic observed"; sid:1000204; rev:1;)

# 5. Detect HTTPS traffic
alert tcp any any -> any 443 (msg:"HTTPS traffic observed"; sid:1000205; rev:1;)

# 6. Detect traffic to a restricted test port
alert tcp any any -> any 8080 (msg:"Traffic to test port 8080"; sid:1000206; rev:1;)

# 7. Detect UDP traffic on a test port
alert udp any any -> any 5353 (msg:"UDP traffic on test port 5353"; sid:1000207; rev:1;)
```

These rules detect traffic. They do not establish whether the firewall allowed it. You must compare the observations with the firewall's expected policy.

Test the rules against a capture:

```bash
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.lua \
-R /etc/snort/firewall.rules \
-r firewall-test.pcap \
-A alert_fast
```

The official Snort documentation explains live interface monitoring, PCAP analysis and inline operation. Snort 3 Rule Writing Guide +1

### 5\. Test individual protocols

Use a separate machine or a controlled test server in your lab.

| Protocol | Port       | Test purpose                 |
| -------- | ---------- | ---------------------------- |
| HTTP     | TCP 80     | Web access policy            |
| HTTPS    | TCP 443    | Encrypted web access policy  |
| SSH      | TCP 22     | Remote administration policy |
| DNS      | UDP/TCP 53 | Name resolution policy       |
| FTP      | TCP 21     | File transfer policy         |
| SMTP     | TCP 25     | Email transmission policy    |
| NTP      | UDP 123    | Time synchronisation policy  |
| SMB      | TCP 445    | File sharing policy          |
| RDP      | TCP 3389   | Remote desktop policy        |
| QUIC     | UDP 443    | HTTP/3 traffic policy        |
| ICMP     | Protocol 1 | Ping and diagnostic policy   |

For example, check a permitted or restricted test service:

```bash
nc -vz 192.168.1.20 22
```

Check HTTPS:

```bash
curl -I --connect-timeout 5 https://example.com
```

Check DNS:

```bash
dig example.com
```

Check ICMP:

```bash
ping -c 4 192.168.1.20
```

For UDP, use an appropriate test service and verify the result on the receiving machine. A UDP timeout alone does not prove that the firewall blocked the traffic.

### 6\. Test port accessibility with Nmap

Nmap complements Snort by actively checking whether services are reachable.

Install it:

```bash
sudo apt install nmap -y
```

Check selected TCP ports on your authorised test machine:

```bash
nmap -sT -Pn -p 22,80,443,445,3389 192.168.1.20
```

Check selected UDP ports:

```bash
sudo nmap -sU -Pn -p 53,123,161 192.168.1.20
```

Interpretation:

| Result        | Meaning                                                                |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Open          | Service is accepting connections                                       |
| Closed        | Host responded, but service is not listening                           |
| Filtered      | Nmap could not determine the state, often because filtering intervened |
| Open/filtered | UDP response was insufficient to distinguish the state                 |

Do not interpret `filtered` as definitive proof of correct firewall enforcement.

### 7\. Important firewall bypass checks

These are particularly important during a security assessment.

| Check                | What to verify                                          |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| IPv4 versus IPv6     | Are both protected by equivalent policies?              |
| Inbound connections  | Are unauthorised incoming connections rejected?         |
| Outbound connections | Can restricted applications communicate externally?     |
| DNS policy           | Can clients use only authorised DNS resolvers?          |
| VPN policy           | Are unauthorised VPN connections restricted?            |
| Network segmentation | Can guest devices reach protected servers?              |
| NAT rules            | Are port-forwarding rules exposing unintended services? |
| Fragmented packets   | Are fragmented packets inspected consistently?          |
| Stateful inspection  | Are invalid or unexpected connection states rejected?   |
| Firewall logging     | Are denied connections recorded?                        |

For IPv6, check:

```bash
ip -6 addr
```

Check IPv6 firewall rules:

```bash
sudo ufw status verbose
```

If using nftables:

```bash
sudo nft list ruleset
```

A firewall that protects IPv4 but leaves IPv6 unrestricted can expose services unexpectedly.

### 8\. How to determine whether traffic actually bypassed the firewall

This is the most important part of the exercise.

![Blinding Snort IDS/IPS: Breaking the Modbus OT Preprocessor | Claroty](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/YHYV1aR6GEmvYnJodhvrvPds7GNWmK54oCAg873zoPmRdrqxQzfbY_prQVM5TXYBhbSnNcI-CWHJy7VEgmHSis3LmmNYUcr6hBikHAzJmxLUuUp5EY5lfxJ8xZChxINUPHDAKiw3Odg7HGBLOezjPoyVLFDPx7cSkVcYnD0dXc0F5BmMPXr8ghqEa9Q9t-Jv?purpose=fullsize)

Ideally, capture traffic at two points:

* Outside the firewall.

* Inside the firewall.

Compare the results.

| Outside sensor   | Inside sensor                       | Interpretation                                                              |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Traffic observed | Traffic observed                    | Traffic crossed the firewall                                                |
| Traffic observed | No matching traffic                 | Firewall may have blocked it, or another network device may have dropped it |
| No traffic       | Traffic observed                    | Investigate alternate paths, capture placement and local traffic            |
| Traffic observed | Traffic observed on prohibited port | Potential policy violation requiring investigation                          |

Also compare:

* Firewall logs.

* Snort alerts.

* Nmap results.

* Server application logs.

* Network routing and NAT configurations.

A single Snort alert cannot establish a firewall bypass.

